 "notes": "The trip-record reproduction uses the 38 MTA CBD taxi zones for both pickup and drop-off, weekday pickups from 8AM to 6PM, federal holidays excluded, and total miles over total hours. It gives 6.94, 6.95 and 6.62 mph for FY24, FY25 and FY26 against the MMR's 6.9, 6.9 and 6.5. The parquet files match the NYC Open Data copies of 2024 and 2025 (rwwi-khbc, irx2-vgm4) exactly in trip count for most months of 2024 and within 0.31% for every month; the 2025 Open Data copy holds slightly fewer trips from April 2025 on, which suggests a later revision of one of the two copies. The MTA series 6p29-6xqn (all hours, taxi and FHV) ends in August 2025 and runs about 1.7 mph higher (8.57 and 8.66 mph in FY24 and FY25), so it is not the MMR's source. Reconciliation with mob-crz-traffic. The MMR number has no comparison area and uses fiscal years. FY25 holds six untolled months (July to December 2024, 6.66 mph under R1) and six tolled months (January to June 2025, 7.24 mph), so the toll's effect is averaged with the slow pre-toll autumn and FY25 equals FY24. FY26 holds the second half of 2025 (6.66 mph) and January to June 2026 (6.57 mph). In January to June 2026 taxi speeds fell everywhere in the city: by 9.2% in the CBD, 5.9% in Manhattan north of 60th Street and 6.5% in the rest of the city against January to June 2025, and the fall appears for both taxi vendors (exploratory check). The fall in the MMR number from 6.9 to 6.5 mph is therefore a citywide fall, not a fall in the CBD relative to elsewhere. Relative to the rest of the city the CBD gained: the ratio of CBD to rest-of-city speed rose 5.1% from calendar 2024 to calendar 2025, close to the +5.7% of mob-crz-traffic, and in January to June 2026 it was still 2.9% above January to June 2024. Relative to Manhattan north of 60th Street, the gain was 2.6% in January to June 2025 and had disappeared by the second half of 2025 (-0.5% against the second half of 2024) and January to June 2026 (-1.0% against January to June 2024). The two published results are both reproduced by the same trip records; they answer different questions, and the choice of comparison area changes the size and persistence of the effect. These comparison-area figures are descriptive outputs named in the registration's plan, not tests.\n\nAfter the referee (13 Nov 2026): the referee rebuilt R1 from the 36 trip files and obtained 6.949 and 6.617 mph, identical to the outcome. Fiscal 2026 reproduces to 0.12 mph, which rounds to 6.6 and not to the published 6.5. The method is not identified: five of the nine registered readings pass, and the 0.2 mph tolerance is half of the 0.4 mph change the indicator reports. Ten of eleven further trip filters stay within tolerance (fiscal 2026 from 6.44 to 6.70); standard-rate trips or positive fares give 6.44 and 6.87, which round to 6.4 and 6.9. A jackknife over days gives a standard error of 0.05 mph, so R1 is 2.3 standard errors from the stated fiscal 2026 value and is accepted because of the registered tolerance. The share of vendor 1, whose trips are 0.1 to 0.3 mph slower, fell from 25% to 19% of trips, and a change in the trip records is not ruled out as part of the fiscal 2026 fall; weather in February 2026 was not examined. The registration has no statement of which data the registrant had seen; the MTA speed series fetched earlier that day runs 1.7 mph higher and could not have been used to tune R1.",
